A||||10||jelly2m8||Fierostore 88 lowering springs||||1||
Z||000000||jelly2m8||12-27-2001||12:18 AM||||Anybody use the Fierostore 88 lowering springs?<P> How do like them, and the over all quality?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||AkursedX||12-27-2001||12:46 AM||||I have them on my 88 along with poly and KYB's all around.  <P>All in all, I think they are a greatproduct.  They dropped the car about one inch all around and gave it a great look.<P>The ride is really harsh though, especially on city streets, but the handling is phenominal.<P>I have heard some negatives about them though from some people on this forum, like too much dropping until they were riding on bumpstops, but I never faced this problem.<P>I say to buy them, it will make your car's appearance alot better.<P>------------------<BR><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Standard||12-27-2001||03:22 AM||||Paul used them on the front of my coupe.. pretty harsh ride.  Definatly dropped the car lower than an inch, too.  <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/quad_sig_pic_1.jpg">        <BR><FONT COLOR="red">1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed</FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1"><FONT COLOR="white">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="blue">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||pherder||12-27-2001||09:14 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Standard:<BR><B>Paul used them on the front of my coupe.. pretty harsh ride.  Definatly dropped the car lower than an inch, too.  <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>More like a 2" drop in the front.<P>Paul<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||L67Formula||12-27-2001||09:59 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pherder:<BR><B> More like a 2" drop in the front.<P>Paul</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Are you getting any tire rub with the 2" drop?<BR>thanks, jp<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||pherder||12-27-2001||11:06 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by L67Formula:<BR><B> Are you getting any tire rub with the 2" drop?<BR>thanks, jp<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Standard will have to answer that.  I built the car and sold it to Standard.<P>I don't remember any wheel rub but the car had 205R60x15 in front (i.e. the stock size) with the 15x6 88 GT/Formula wheels.<P>Paul||||reg||1
Z||000006||blakeinspace||12-27-2001||04:25 PM||||Put in all poly bushings, KYB and new '88 springs (Fierostore) in the fall... MUCH stiffer ride (but I like it) and it lowered my car about an inch.  No tire rub, and it does look a lot better.  It is amazing how much lower that inch is when you get in and out of the car!  I did trim the rubber stops about 3/4" just in case though.  The rear dropped a little more than the front did, but when I am in the car it looks level.  Your post reminds me... I was going to post a question about Fierostore poly endlinks...<BR>Catch me up there.  I also thought that American Custom Engineering or West Coast Fiero offer lowering springs for the '88, but I am not sure though.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Standard||12-27-2001||04:53 PM||||No tire rub that I'm aware of.  If I ever get a bunch of extra money it's gonna have coil-overs and tubular control arms in the front  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||nyguy12180||12-27-2001||09:13 PM||||I have the Fiero Store springs on the rear of my "88 Mera--lowred it about an inch, with no noticeable problems at all. The quality is very good. Been on there for over 4 years with no problems. By the way, i used dropped spindles on the front--an excellent, albeit expensive ,way to go.  I got them from the defunct Ryanne Motorsports. I believe that Held carries them now.||||reg||1
Z||000009||jelly2m8||12-27-2001||09:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by nyguy12180:<BR><B>I have the Fiero Store springs on the rear of my "88 Mera--lowred it about an inch, with no noticeable problems at all. The quality is very good. Been on there for over 4 years with no problems. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR> Thats the type of reply I was looking for.<P>Right now I'm at a 3 way toss up between the Fierostores, ACE's 1-1/4" drop, and cutting my stock springs.<P> I think I will go with the Fierostores, and keep the stock springs uncut for back up's in case I don't like the lowering springs.<P>Thanks a bunch<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Spiff||12-27-2001||10:47 PM||||I had my springs, Kyb's and poly bushings installed in September. I have had no problems yet, except that my muffler now scrapes at times. That because the PO put a new exhaust that sits lower than normal. That will get fixed when I install the Borla exhaust next week.But really no problems at all.<BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/spiffsig2_1.gif">      <P>1988 Fiero Formula  T-Top  # 1008 of 1252  (5 Spd White/ Grey)<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:spiff@yorkhost.com">spiff@yorkhost.com</A>    <BR><B>Exterior</B><BR>17" O.Z. Nova/1" Drop springs/Kyb and Poly all around<BR><B>Interior</B><BR>Carbon Fiber/White face gauges/Custon horn and door tabs<BR>2" Roll Bar/ACC Carpets/Mr. Mikes (Comming Soon)<BR><B>Engine</B><BR>Darrell Morse Strut tower brace,TB and Plenum/Borla Exhaust||||reg||1
